From eea977de2a2dd1a0d14bebc3bcd7cbaa4d967894 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 03:08:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] system: choose which internal disks are shown

"Internal partitions that are shown" leaves partitions unmounted, and a
partition that is not mounted is still on its disk, where a program that
runs with full rights can read it. Someone who wants the disk of Windows out
of reach and the disks of their films in sight had no way to have both.

Add "Internal disks that are shown" under the switch, before the list of
partitions, shown only while the switch is on. It lists the NVMe and SATA
disks of this start, as /usr/lib/libreelec/internal-disk-choice lists them,
with their model, size, the end of their serial number and the names of
their devices now; the disk that this system is on is not offered. The first
line of the list is for any disk that is not in it (a new one, another PC);
with no list yet, everything is ticked, as the system does today. What is not
ticked is kept away from the kernel from the next start: at each start that
shows the internal disks the init lets go of it before anything else runs,
tells the kernel to attach no other internal disk until the next start and
locks the kernel down. The choice is saved by the same script, which the
init asks; nothing of it is kept here. A disk that the init kept away in this
start is offered by what names it, and said to be kept away.

Before a list keeps disks away for the first time, a text that is read at
one's own pace says what that means: not even a program with full rights
reaches them; no other internal disk is attached until the next start; the
kernel runs with its lock-down, so that the drivers that add-ons bring are
not loaded; a disk without a serial number is kept away too, and every disk
when the list cannot be read; the disk of the system never is; it does not
hold against a program that changes the list or the boot menu and waits for
the next start; it holds on every computer. Then it asks, with "No"
preselected. Disks that cannot be told apart, and internal disks of a kind
that cannot be kept away (behind a RAID or SAS controller, eMMC), are said
by the names of their devices. A list that cannot be read, now or when the
init read it, is said, with the offer to take it away ("No" preselected):
that list keeps every internal disk away. In a start that hides the internal
disks there is nothing to list, and that is said. A list that is closed, or
Kodi stopping, changes nothing; a list that keeps nothing away is taken away.

At the start of Kodi, where the init found the list unreadable, or kept disks
away without the kernel holding them off (no switch, no lock-down), a
notification says so.

The text under the switch, the explanation shown before it is turned on and
the help of the partitions say that disks can be kept away, and that the
kernel then runs with its lock-down. The script is run with a PATH and a
locale of its own, not in the environment of Kodi. The texts are in English,
and in Spanish for Argentina ("vos"), Spain and Mexico.
